A||N||15||2xsess||DIS Help||||5||
Z||000000||2xsess||12-11-2000||07:46 PM||||I somehow manged to volunteer myself to look at this womans 88 coupe. I haven't actually made it to her home to look at the car yet. I wanted to tell you what info she gave me and see if anyone has any ideas. I'm not familiar with the DIS engines at all. Basically I wanted to go over there with a little knowledge. I read through Ogres great cave which gave me some insight.<BR>Here's the info she gave me. <BR>The car has been in her family floating from her father to her uncle and then to her. When she bought it she said it wouldn't idle at a stop so someone messed with a screw on the TB and it idled better. Then one day it wouldn't start. Her husband took the head off and cleaned out all the oil build-up and replaced it with new gaskets. Now the car still won't run and she claims that it shoots 18" flames out of the TB,if it doesn't actually shoot flames, it backfires through the TB. Now like I said I haven't actually seen this yet so I can't attest to it shooting flames but I'll take her word for it until I see it. <BR>Hopefully soemone has had this problem before and can help me go there with knowledge instead of staring at it and shaking my head. <P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-11-2000||07:52 PM||||Check for bad or crossed plug wires. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-11-2000||07:58 PM||||It's hard telling what's wrong with it now that hubby worked on it. Check for spark and 1.5 foot long flames.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-11-2000||08:56 PM||||<B>Warning: if you are going to spark test it use a spare plug set to 060 gap or get a spark testing fixture. DIS pumps way more current than HEI. It can kill you if you are holding on it when it fires.</B><P>plug wires are likely wrong. 1&4 to bottom coil and 2&3 to top one. is the first thing to check. It only matters that the plug pairs are on the right coil. doesn't matter which way they are on it.<P>The TBI is covered in the Haynes books on fuel injection. If you get the one that covers 87 you'll have all the injection systems used in Fiero and most other GM cars.<P>When it fired up the intake it cooked the MAT sensor. You'll have to replace that or you'll likely get codes 44&45 at odd times. Make sure the new one has the plastic cage. Borg Warner makes the correct one. Not all others do.<P>You'll need to look for vac leaks and all that mess. check the PCV elbows. they like to come off or leak.<P>I have an additional article on the 87-88 motor I'll email the text of. I'm not finished with it yet so it's not in the cave. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||12-11-2000||09:06 PM||||Thanks, I got your email Ogre. Hopefully I'll get to look at it this week. <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||12-11-2000||11:56 PM||||I beg to differ on the statement<P>plug wires are likely wrong. 1&4 to bottom coil and 2&3 to top one. is the first thing to check. It only matters that the plug pairs are on the right coil. doesn't matter which way they are on it.<P>I've checked three different GM service manuals which cover the 2.5 engines VIN's U, R & E with the DIS system installed they all say the same thing<P>QUOTE:<BR>The DIRECT INGITION SYSTEM (DIS) is a distributorless ignition system installed on the 1987-88 2.5L L4 engines (LR8). The (DIS) system uses individual coils (2), crankshaft sensor, iginition module and electronic control module (ECM) to directly transfer spark to the spark plugs without the use of a distributor assembly. No vacuum or mechanical advances are used.<BR>The (DIS) system uses a "Waste Spark" method of spark distribution. Each cylinder is paired with its opposing cylinder in the firing order, so that one cylinder fires on the compression stroke and the other on the exhaust stroke. Most of the voltage is used for the compression stroke because little voltage is needed on the exhaust stroke. This dual firing helps reduce exhaust emissions.<P>So,.... from the bottom to top the correct placement for the correct firing order is as follows:<BR>         1 .. .. ..4 . .. ..3 . .. .. 2<P>If these are correct You may wish to check the MAT sensor. On a personal note I'd suspect either one or both of the coils for shorts and the perhaps the crank sensor (I had one split before and it seemed like all the plugs lit up at the same time!).<BR>To bad you don't have access to a scan tool so you could monitor as you crank, really helps to narrow it down in a hurry!<P>Good luck!<P>Might also be the module too but I don't think so with out being there.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||dman||12-12-2000||04:18 PM||||Hello everyone. I don't mean any offense here so please take my viewpoint as exactly that...a viewpoint.<P>I think theogre is absolutely correct. It makes no difference which terminal the plug wire is placed on as long as it is on the right coil pack. 1&4 on the proper coil pack, and 2&3 on the other.<P>It is because of the waste spark system that it doesn't matter. When a coil pack fires, both terminals are at the same voltage potential. The cylinder that is on the exhaust stroke has no (low) compression so the waste spark energy consumed is actually very little. Most of the energy flows to the opposite terminal, the cylinder that actually needs the spark.<P>Switch the wires around and try it yourself. You'll see there is no difference. This of course applies only to DIS systems.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||05:46 PM||||Going back to the original problem of stalling at stop signs, it might be the solenoid if its an automatic or possibly a jammed EGR valve. I have also had to change the module underneath the DIS packs after 260,000 miles after it had no spark even after putting known coil packs on. Couldn't complain of not getting service life out of the module!<P>We had a coupe that was stalling and running poorly and couldn't figure out why until we checked the EGR diaphram, it would stick when we manually pushed it. To test we put a known good EGR valve and were astonished at the difference.<P>Good luck to ya!||||reg||2
Z||000008||theogre||12-12-2000||07:22 PM||||Yeah, stalling pulling up to a stop, especially when the tranny is warmed up, is likely the TC lock solinoid. There's notes about that in my cave to.<P>As long as the plug pairs are on the correct coil it makes no diferance. I've tested it. It works. I was in a hurry changing a fried coil. The 1 & 4 plugs were backwards for over a month. Car ran just fine. It only works for waste spark DIS.<P>You're right about the crank sensor. That thing could also do this if it's throwing crap at the ignition module.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2xsess||12-12-2000||09:10 PM||||Well, I got a bit of time to go look at the car after work tonight.<P>The reason her husband took the head off was because oil was coming up through the pcv. <BR>He said he took it all apart and cleaned it and changed the valve seals. <BR>When I got there the battery was dead so we jumped it off my car and cranked it over. <BR>I didn't wait to see the flames, I could see the fuel spraying out of the trottle body. I checked the plug wires and 3 and 4 were crossed. My Haynes says that the far right plug is 1, I went by that. I didn't take anything apart tonight as I was losing daylight quickly. <P>Anymore thoughts?<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||09:52 PM||||With 3 and 4 corrected, did it start?<P>Dyin' to know!<P>Also, if the PCV valve is on the valve cover, where there is a lot of oil, isn't it normal for some oil residue to come out? <P>It would take a lot more than that to get me to take off the head...<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||12-12-2000||10:05 PM||||I think I'll stick with the GM Electronic Engine Controls Manual myself. Not been let down yet.<BR>Are you talking litterly when you said 'fuel was spraying "out" of the throttle body'?<BR>Might be a bad 'O' ring around the injector allowing a vast amount of additional fuel into the engine. This will cause it not to idle or stay running, maybe detonation of excess fuel in the intake? I'm not sure if it could cause an increase of crankcase pressure to vent oil though.||||reg||1
Z||000012||2xsess||12-12-2000||10:55 PM||||Oops. I forgot to say that it sounded like it wanted to start but it just cranked. <P>Yes I could see a spray of fuel coming from the throttle body.<P>As for the firing order, I followed the numbers on the coil packs. (hopefully that will quell this little dispute here <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )I don't know about all this waste spark stuff so I was glad the numbers were on the coils.<P>The guy(John) said that there was so much oil coming into the air cleaner box that the filter was soaked.<P>I assume that if it actually shoots flames then it has spark, I'm rather leery about testing for spark. Not into burnt hair and all that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I want to thank you guys, I knew nothing about the DIS before you all educated me. Thanks, hopefully with your knowledge and my hands we can get this coupe on the road again. <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||2xsess||12-17-2000||05:58 PM||||I went over this afternoon to attempt getting this car running.<P>I checked the plugs out, they were coated in oil and smelled of gas. Two of them were loose too. I cleaned them off and checked the gap on them and reinstalled. <BR>Then I removed the DIS brick to inspect the crank position sensor. It was whole but covered in gunk, I cleaned it off and put it back in. Is it supposed to have dielectric grease on it before install? <BR>I checked the computer for codes and there wasn't any. <BR>So we jumped it off and it still only popped through the TB. Every once ina while it would kind of sound like it wanted to start. <BR>I also found out that someone messed with a screw on the TB. It's on the side of the TB facing the trunk. I'm not sire what that screw does. Anyone know if messing with that screw could cause these problems?<BR>The husband told me that a shop had told them the car needed a head job, could this be the cause?<P>TIA<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||12-17-2000||11:33 PM||||Yes, fooling with the idle stop screw can make it not start.<P>Oil on the plugs is a bad sign. They need to be replaced.<P>dielectric should be applied to the contacts for the CPS but for your needs right now it's a non issue. The CPS will need more work later if it ever starts. likely the O ring is bad and it will leak once the car is running.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||2xsess||12-17-2000||11:38 PM||||I told them to save their money and take it to a shop. I'm afraid to get too into it since it's not my car and not my cash.<P>I also told them to let me know first if they decide to sell it so that I could offer it to one of you all here.<P>Thanks for all your advice guys<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
